// Lesson 2.5 — Physics & Stats Studio: Catch Up and Tune
// Studio day: the settle-and-tune day Unit 2's weight earns. No new API. The
// student finishes 2.1-2.4 if they are behind, re-tunes the movement numbers
// now that a Rigidbody2D does the moving, and fixes the top item from their
// 2.4 playtest notes.
//
// WHY THIS STUDIO EXISTS. 2.2 replaced `transform.Translate(movement)` with
// `body.linearVelocity`, so every distance-per-second number selected in 1.5
// was tuned for a body that no longer moves the player. The whole point of the
// day is that a speed number is not a distance once mass and drag sit between
// the number and the motion — so the tune is a re-measure, not a re-read.
//
// NO LADDER LEVEL, ON PURPOSE. This is a studio day, not a build lesson: the
// page declares no ask banner and no Break It Down block, because
// src/data/asks.ts has no entry for 2.5 and verify-site fails any lesson that
// declares a ladder level without one. So this page carries the editor/code
// split, the pane names and the call naming, and nothing else — the same
// treatment 6.5 and 8.6 got as converted studio days.
//
// GROUND TRUTH (from 2.1-2.4): health, coins and the max-health cap live on
// PlayerController; StatManager is still the empty stub it ships as, and stays
// empty until the 5.3 refactor. Movement is `body.linearVelocity`, which is
// units per second, so the Rigidbody2D's Mass and Linear Drag are part of how
// far a speed value actually carries. Spikes are solid (Is Trigger off) and the
// coin and potion are triggers; the death rule is the student's own choice from
// 2.2 (reload by index, reload by name, or reset and move without reloading).
//
// The single socratic block is the page's lab form. Its three questions are the
// only answer boxes here, so no positional id is at risk.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>2.5 — Physics &amp; Stats Studio: Catch Up and Tune</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Unit 2 is the heaviest unit of the semester, and it arrived in one week: a physics body, a
    coin, a spike, a death rule and a health cap. Today it settles. Finish what is unfinished,
    then re-tune your movement — because <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a> handed the moving to a
    Rigidbody2D, and every distance-per-second number you chose in <a href="1.5.html">1.5</a> was
    chosen for a body that no longer moves you.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Editor</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Three jobs, in this order: finish the unit, then tune, then fix one thing.</strong>
        Tuning before the unit is finished tunes a game that is still changing underneath you.
    </p>

    <ol>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy → Inspector →</strong> open the Player and confirm Unit 2's parts are
            all present: the <strong>Rigidbody 2D</strong> from <a href="2.1.html">2.1</a>, and the
            movement script from <a href="1.4.html">1.4</a> and <a href="1.5.html">1.5</a>. If the
            body is missing, that is the catch-up job — <a href="2.1.html">2.1</a> adds it, and
            <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a> replaces the old movement line with
            <code>body.linearVelocity = direction * moveSpeed;</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Inspector → Rigidbody 2D →</strong> read <strong>Body Type</strong>,
            <strong>Mass</strong>, <strong>Linear Drag</strong> and <strong>Gravity Scale</strong>.
            These are the fields that decide what the world does back to your number.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Game view →</strong> play one straight line on flat ground for three seconds and
            measure how far the player gets. Compare it to the same test in <a href="1.5.html">1.5</a>.
            Change exactly one field, then measure again — two fields changed and one measurement is
            a guess, not a tuning pass.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Scene view →</strong> if any of 2.1-2.4 is unfinished, finish it here. The coin's
            trigger and tag (<a href="2.1.html">2.1</a>), the spike's solid collider and the death
            rule (<a href="2.2.html">2.2</a>), the potion and its cap (<a href="2.3.html">2.3</a>),
            the prefab and the spawner (<a href="2.4.html">2.4</a>).
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Game view →</strong> fix the item at the <em>top</em> of your
            <a href="2.4.html">2.4</a> playtest notes, not the one that is easiest. Then play the
            beat that item is about and confirm the change did what you claimed.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>GitHub Desktop →</strong> commit with a message that names the field you changed
            and the number you chose, so the next tune has something to compare against.
        </li>
    </ol>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Knob</th><th>What moving it changes</th><th>What you measure</th></tr>
        <tr><td><code>moveSpeed</code></td><td>How far the player travels each second.</td><td>Distance covered in three seconds on flat ground.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rigidbody 2D → <strong>Mass</strong></td><td>How hard a solid push shoves the player around.</td><td>How far a spike or wall moves you at the same approach speed.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rigidbody 2D → <strong>Linear Drag</strong></td><td>How quickly the player stops once input stops.</td><td>Seconds between releasing the keys and standing still.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rigidbody 2D → <strong>Gravity Scale</strong></td><td>Whether the body falls at all. In the top-down Overworld it is 0.</td><td>Whether the player drifts downward over time — it should not.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>health</code>'s starting value</td><td>How many hits the player survives.</td><td>Hits taken before the death rule fires.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>The potion's heal amount (<a href="2.3.html">2.3</a>)</td><td>How much one potion restores before the cap stops it.</td><td>Health after two potions taken at the maximum.</td></tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>A speed number is only true for the body it was tuned on.</strong> In
        <a href="1.5.html">1.5</a> you moved the Transform directly, so <code>moveSpeed</code> was
        the whole story. In <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a> the Rigidbody2D took over the moving, and it
        consults mass and drag on the way. The same number now produces a different distance — which
        is why today is a re-measure, not a re-read of last month's notes.
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Code</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Nothing new gets written today. Two decisions from this unit live in a file rather than in a
        field, and a tune is the moment to check you can still say what they are.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Specification: where the numbers live.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>Name the file and the field that holds each of these: the movement speed, the current health, the coin count, and the maximum health. Say them from memory first, then open the file and check.</li>
            <li><code>StatManager</code> is still the empty stub it shipped as. <a href="2.1.html">2.1</a> showed you the alternative: a number can live on the object that persists, or on the object that disappears. Where does health live today, and which of those two is it?</li>
            <li>If you moved health into <code>StatManager</code> now, it would survive a scene change. Is that a feature this game wants yet, or is it the job the <a href="5.3.html">5.3</a> refactor exists to do? What would moving it early cost you this week?</li>
            <li>The cap from <a href="2.3.html">2.3</a> is a rule, not arithmetic. Which form did you use — a plain <code>if</code>, <code>Mathf.Min(a, b)</code>, or <code>Mathf.Clamp(value, min, max)</code> — and which of them says "this value has a ceiling" most plainly to the next person who reads it?</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Specification: the death rule.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>What does dying actually do in your build — reload the scene by index, reload a scene by name, or reset the player without reloading? State it in one sentence without opening Unity.</li>
            <li>If you reload, everything on <code>PlayerController</code> resets, the coin count included. Is that the rule you meant to choose in <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a>, and can you defend it — or did you inherit it by accident?</li>
            <li>The spike reports a collision and the coin reports an overlap. Which of the two needs <strong>Is Trigger</strong> ticked, and what goes wrong in your build if you tick the wrong one?</li>
            <li>Where does the player reappear after dying, and is that the same place a spawned coin from <a href="2.4.html">2.4</a> would land? If those two answers differ, decide which is correct and say why.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Tuning Is a Measurement</h3>
        <ol>
<li>You changed one field and the player now moves the way you wanted. Which did you actually change — the number, or the body underneath it — and how would you tell the difference from the Game view alone?</li>
<li>Tuning one variable at a time means one measurement per change. Why does changing Mass and Linear Drag together and measuring once teach you nothing about which of them did the work?</li>
<li>Unit 2 is the heaviest unit in the semester and it earned a day to settle. In CSP terms (CRD-2, testing and refinement), what is the difference between "the game works" and "the game works at a value I chose on purpose"?</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Say whether 2.1-2.4 are finished, and finish any that are not</li>
        <li>Re-measure your movement as a distance rather than a number, and name the Rigidbody 2D field that changed it</li>
        <li>Name the file and field behind all four values: speed, health, coins, max health</li>
        <li>State your death rule in one sentence and say what resets when it fires</li>
        <li>Fix exactly one item from your playtest notes, then verify that it worked</li>
        <li>Commit and push</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/index.html">Unit 2: Stats &amp; Pickups</a> — the whole unit this studio settles</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/triggers-vs-collisions.html">Triggers vs Collisions</a> — the trigger-versus-solid decision, in full</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — Rigidbody2D fields, <code>Mathf.Clamp(value, min, max)</code> and the physics callbacks</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="2.6.html">2.6 — Unit 2 Review + Second Playtest</a>, where a new partner plays the fixed build and the two sheets get compared.</p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="2.5" title="2.5 Physics &amp; Stats Studio: Catch Up and Tune — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
